<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isma'il ibn Ahmad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/849">849</a>–<a href="/years/907">907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Ismail Samani, Ismail I of the Samanids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isma'il ibn Ahmad, known as Ismail Samani, was the Samanid emir who made his family the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and Khurasan. Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> under his brother Nasr, he defeated the Saffarid Amr ibn al-Layth in 900 and ruled from Bukhara as governor for the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, with wide autonomy. His mausoleum in Bukhara is a masterpiece of early Islamic architecture, and he is honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> as a founder of Tajik statehood.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-ibn-ahmad.jpg" alt="Isma'il ibn Ahmad" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ninara from Helsinki, Finland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modern_Center_of_Dushanbe,_Tajikistan_(43804970971).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ismail was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Farghana</a> in May 849, the son of Ahmad ibn Asad and a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saman-khuda">Saman Khuda</a>, the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>. The Samanids were native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>. The family claimed descent from the Parthian Mihran family, possibly to enhance its lineage, and spoke and advocated Persian and used pre-Islamic bureaucratic titles, probably to suggest that its rule continued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasr-i-samanid">Nasr I</a> ascended the throne in 864/5 and sent Ismail to take control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, which had been devastated by looting by forces from Khwarezm. The citizens welcomed him as someone who could bring stability.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A disagreement over the distribution of tax money led to a struggle between the brothers, which Ismail won. He did not formally overthrow Nasr, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> had given Nasr the investiture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> had claims there. He recognised Nasr until his death in August 892 and then took power officially. In 893 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taraz">Taraz</a>, the capital of the Karluk Turks, with many slaves and livestock, and a Nestorian church there was converted into a mosque. He also ended the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usrushana">Ushrusana</a> and partitioned Khwarezm.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> urged Ismail to fight the Saffarid ruler Amr-i Laith, whom he considered a usurper. The armies met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> in the spring of 900. Ismail came out with 20,000 horsemen against Amr's 70,000, many of them poorly equipped, but Amr was captured after some of his troops switched sides. The caliph invested Ismail with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, Ray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>. A Samanid army defeated the Zaydid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-zayd">Muhammad ibn Zayd</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a> in 900, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> took over Tabaristan, restoring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> there. Ismail sent gifts to the caliph but paid no tribute and never took a title higher than amir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After a long sickness Ismail died on 24 November 907 and was succeeded by his son Ahmad Samani. He gave away enormous booty and kept nothing. Because of his campaigns in the north, his realm was so safe from incursions that the defences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> went unused, which was regretted when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a> and others later attacked. A historian of Bukhara writing in 943 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-al-mulk">Nizam al-Mulk</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyasatnama">Siyasatnama</a>, both praised his justice. After the end of Soviet rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> his legacy was revived: the currency, the somoni, is named after him, and the highest mountain of Tajikistan, once called Stalin Peak and Communism Peak, is now Ismoil Somoni Peak.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ismail Samani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nasr ibn Ahmad unified their holdings, and his brother <b>Isma'il ibn Ahmad</b> defeated the Saffarids in 900, ruling Transoxiana and Khurasan as Abbasid governors.</blockquote><a href="/state/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>The afshins continued as local governors under Abbasid and Samanid authority until 893, when the Samanid Empire's <b>Isma'il ibn Ahmad</b> ended their dynasty and absorbed Ustrushana.</blockquote><a href="/state/ustrushana">Ustrushana</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fergana, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.39,71.79&amp;year=886" aria-label="Fergana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6313 720 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Isma'il ibn Ahmad"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6763 990h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6763 990h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.39,71.79&amp;year=886">What was here in 886 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
